SOLUTIONS={
1:('DIG-01','Two hundred and five thousand is 205000. Add one hundred and fifty, 150, to obtain 205150.'),
2:('FRAC-01','The picture has nine equal squares and four shaded corner squares. The shaded fraction is 4/9.'),
3:('DIG-01','Subtract 2000 first: 2220 − 2000 = 220. Then subtract the remaining 22: 220 − 22 = 198.'),
4:('DIG-01','Write the decimals with two places: 0.08, 0.40 and 1.03. Eight hundredths is less than forty hundredths, and both are less than one whole. The increasing order is 0.08, 0.4, 1.03.'),
5:('ALG-02','The age gap is 7 − 3 = 4 years and stays constant. When the brother is 7, the older child is 7 + 4 = 11.'),
6:('GEO-01','Follow the boundary, including every inward notch. The outline has four identical quarters. Each quarter contains 13 unit edges, giving 4 × 13 = 52 cm. As a check, count shared edges between the 29 squares: there are 32, so the perimeter is 4 × 29 − 2 × 32 = 52.'),
7:('DATA-03','The needle points to 2.4 on a scale labelled ×1000 rpm. Multiply: 2.4 × 1000 = 2400 revolutions per minute. The scale factor is essential.'),
8:('RATE-01','One litre is 1000 mL. After drinking 320 mL, the amount left is 1000 − 320 = 680 mL.'),
9:('GEO-02','Area is length times width. P: 12 × 2 = 24; Q: 8 × 3 = 24; R: 6 × 4 = 24; S: 24 × 1 = 24. All four rectangles have area 24 cm².'),
10:('RATE-01','The activities take 15 + 10 + 20 + 5 + 30 = 80 minutes. Work backwards from 8:55 am by one hour and twenty minutes: 7:35 am is the latest shower start.'),
11:('DATA-01','Twice as likely means the red area must be twice the white area. Spinner D has four equal red sectors and two equal white sectors, a ratio of 4:2 = 2:1. The probability of red is 2/3 and white 1/3.'),
12:('DIV-05','Track the endpoints until they stay beyond 4½: 0,3,1,4,2,5,3,6,4,7,5,8,… . The crossings of 4½ occur on 2→5, 5→3, 3→6, 6→4 and 4→7: five crossings. Thereafter even the backward endpoints are at least 5, so it is never crossed again.'),
13:('DIG-05','In the units column, □ + 4 + 1 must end in 1, giving 6 and a carry of 1. The tens column is 4 + □ + 9 + 1, which must be 20, so its missing digit is 6 with a carry of 2. The hundreds column is 3 + □ + 2 = 10, so the final missing digit is 5. Their sum is 6 + 6 + 5 = 17. Check: 46 + 364 + 591 = 1001.'),
14:('GEO-04','From the front, the circle is uppermost, the triangle lies beneath it, and the square is behind both. Turning the whole glued arrangement over reverses this order: the square must be fully visible on top, then the triangle, with the circle behind. It also mirrors the sideways positions. A shows the square on the left and the correct overlap order.'),
15:('ALG-01','The left side is 270 ÷ 45 = 6. The missing number divided by 15 must also equal 6, so it is 6 × 15 = 90. Check: 90 ÷ 15 = 6.'),
16:('GEO-01','The largest square’s side is 32 ÷ 4 = 8 cm. The smallest square’s side is √9 = 3 cm. Their vertical alignment shows that the medium side plus the small side equals 8, so the medium side is 5 cm. Its perimeter is 4 × 5 = 20 cm.'),
17:('FRAC-01','Work backwards. Eight marbles remain after half of the previous remainder was taken, so there had been 16. Those 16 were two-thirds of the original bag. One-third was 8, hence the original total was 3 × 8 = 24 marbles.'),
18:('LOGIC-02','Eight different positive integers have sum at least 1+2+3+4+5+6+7+8 = 36. This lower bound is achievable. Put 1,3,5,7 clockwise around the front face, and put 4,6,8,2 at the corresponding back vertices, matching positions through the cube. Front and back edges join numbers differing by at least 2; the four joining edges have differences 3,3,3,5. No edge joins consecutive numbers, so the minimum sum is 36.'),
19:('RATE-04','The grandchildren’s current total is 27 + 23 + 16 = 66, which is 12 less than 78. Each year their combined age rises by 3 while George’s rises by 1, closing the gap by 2. They become equal after 12 ÷ 2 = 6 years.'),
20:('DATA-02','Multiply each TV count by the number of students reporting it, then add: 0×1 + 1×3 + 2×4 + 3×9 + 4×8 + 5×3 + 6×1 = 0 + 3 + 8 + 27 + 32 + 15 + 6 = 91 televisions. Adding column heights alone would count students, not TVs.'),
21:('FRAC-03','There are 70 + 80 = 150 students. Fifteen percent of the 80 girls is 12; with 6 boys this makes 18 prize winners. The overall percentage is 18/150 × 100 = 12%. Do not average a count and a percentage.'),
22:('GEO-04','Turning the page upside down is a half-turn. Following it with a left-right mirror reflection has the same effect on each letter as reflecting top-to-bottom. Count the letters with horizontal symmetry in the printed style: B,C,D,E,H,I,K,O,X. There are 9. The question concerns individual letter shapes, not whether each stays in its original position on the page.'),
23:('COUNT-05','Count daily student-class places. The students need 1000 × 6 = 6000 places. Each teacher provides 5 classes × 25 students = 125 places. Therefore the number of teachers is 6000 ÷ 125 = 48.'),
24:('COUNT-02','For each fixed first letter, the other three have 3! = 6 orders. Positions 1–6 start with p, 7–12 with q, and 13–18 with r. Position 19 is the first starting with s, followed by the other letters in increasing order: spqr.'),
25:('LOGIC-03','Each straight line totals 40. The left inner circle is 40−7−15−2 = 16. The bottom inner circle is then 40−8−16−9 = 7. Call the upper-right inner circle a, the right inner circle b, and the right outer circle c. The remaining lines give a+c=17, a+b=24 and b+c=31. Adding the last two and subtracting the first gives 2b=38, so b=19. Then a=5 and c=12. The largest of all the entries is 19.')}
